  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Winter Weather Advisories are in effect late tonight through\n   Tuesday for central Idaho, the Bitterroot/Sapphire Mountains,\n   and Southwest Montana.\n\n-  Moderate to heavy precipitation Tuesday morning may bring an\n   earlier transition to a rain/snow mix for Missoula, the\n   Bitterroot, and Seeley Lake Tuesday morning. Accumulations will\n   be limited to grassy/unpaved areas.\n\n-  Widespread overnight freeze-thaw cycles will create patchy ice\n   on valley roadways Tuesday night into Wednesday morning.\n\nLight rain showers will continue through this evening with snow \nlevels holding between 5,000 and 6,000 feet. Forecast models \ncontinue to show snow levels dropping through tonight, bringing \naccumulating snow down to many mountain pass roadways by tomorrow \nmorning.\n\nA more significant storm will arrive late tonight and last through \nearly Wednesday morning, drawing abundant moisture into western \nMontana and north central Idaho. This slow moving system will \ncreate a stalled band of heavy precipitation over western Montana \nand central Idaho. This precipitation band is expected to be \nlocated across the higher terrain of north central Idaho into \nwest-central Montana and may allow snow levels to lower more than \nforecast models indicate. Locations like the Bitterroot Valley, \nMissoula, and Seeley-Swan may see an earlier changeover to a rain \nand snow mix by Tuesday morning, resulting in a trace to one inch \nof slush. Minor winter weather impacts are also possible as far \nnorth as Marias Pass. Bottom line: Tuesday will be a wet and raw \nday for many valley locations, with high temperatures only \nreaching into the mid 30s and persistent rainfall, mixing with \nsnow in the morning hours. Higher elevations will begin to see \nmoderate to heavy snowfall.\n\nPrecipitation will taper off across west-central Montana by \nTuesday afternoon as the heaviest snowfall shifts south into \nLemhi County and southwest Montana, including Butte and Homestake \nPass. Winter Weather Advisories remain in effect for the higher \nterrain of central Idaho, the Bitterroot and Sapphire Mountains, \nand southwest Montana, where plowable snow is anticipated. While \nvalley roads will generally remain wet and slushy during the day \non Tuesday, dropping temperatures Tuesday night will create \nwidespread freezing hazards and patchy ice, particularly on \nbridges and overpasses.\n\nBy Thursday and Friday, a secondary weather system will move into \nthe region. This will maintain cooler temperatures and bring \nadditional accumulating mountain snow, along with a mix of rain \nand snow for valley locations primarily south of the I-90 \ncorridor.",
